      'A convolution layer slides a small filter (also called kernel), for example 3 × 3, over the image. At each position it multiplies the filter numbers by the pixels under them and adds everything up (plus a bias). That one number goes into the output grid, called a feature map. The same filter weights are reused at every position (weight sharing), so the layer finds the same pattern, such as an edge, anywhere in the image.',
