      'A typical CNN repeats [conv, ReLU, (pool)] a few times, then flattens and ends with fully connected layers and a softmax. Early layers learn edges and colour blobs, middle layers textures, deep layers object parts. Each deeper neuron sees a larger region of the original image (bigger receptive field).',
      'Famous architectures: LeNet (1998, conv and pooling then an MLP), AlexNet (2012, deeper and bigger, popularised CNNs, about 60M parameters), VGG (2014, showed depth matters, stacks of 3 × 3 convs), GoogLeNet (Inception modules with parallel 1 × 1, 3 × 3, 5 × 5 filters, 1 × 1 convs to cut channels, about 4M parameters), ResNet (2015, skip connections output F(x) + x, beat human error on ImageNet).',
