      'Text is a sequence: its length varies and word order matters. A normal network needs a fixed-size input, so a simple trick is bag-of-words: count how often each vocabulary word appears. This loses order ("dog bites man" looks the same as "man bites dog"), treats synonyms as unrelated, and gives huge sparse vectors. Embeddings fix the last two problems; sequence models fix the order problem.',
      'A recurrent neural network (RNN) reads one token at a time and carries a hidden state h, its memory: h(t) = tanh(W_x x(t) + W_h h(t-1) + b). The same weights are used at every step, so the number of parameters does not depend on sequence length. Training unrolls the network in time and backpropagates through time. Repeated multiplication by W makes gradients vanish (or explode) over long distances, so long-range dependencies are hard to learn.',
      'An LSTM adds a separate cell state, a conveyor belt of memory, and three gates. Each gate is a sigmoid between 0 (block) and 1 (pass). The forget gate decides how much old memory to keep, the input gate how much of the new candidate to write, and the output gate how much memory to show as h. The update C_t = f × C_(t-1) + i × C̃_t is mostly additive, so information and gradients survive long sequences.',
      'Attention: to build the meaning of a word, take a weighted average of the other words, with bigger weights for more relevant words (in "the river bank", "bank" should look at "river"). Each token produces a query q (what I am looking for), a key k (what I offer) and a value v (the content I pass on), using learned matrices W_Q, W_K, W_V. Score = q⋅k, divide by √d_k, softmax across all keys so the weights sum to 1, output = weighted sum of values. For all tokens at once: Attention(Q, K, V) = softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k)V.',
